    "label": "17",
    "prompt": {
      "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
      "version": 1,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "p29-q17-definitions",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "fontSize": null,
          "align": "left",
          "spaceBefore": 0,
          "spaceAfter": 2,
          "lineHeight": 1.4,
          "indent": 0,
          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Let ",
              "marks": []
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "latex": "S=\\frac{1}{2}(2^x+2^{-x})",
              "display": false
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " and ",
              "marks": []
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "latex": "D=\\frac{1}{2}(2^x-2^{-x})",
              "display": false
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": ".",
              "marks": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p29-q17-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Simplify $SD$, $S+D$, $S-D$ and $S^2-D^2$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$SD=\\frac{1}{4}(2^{2x}-2^{-2x})$, $S+D=2^x$, $S-D=2^{-x}$ and $S^2-D^2=1$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}SD&=\\frac{1}{4}(2^x+2^{-x})(2^x-2^{-x})\\\\SD&=\\frac{1}{4}(2^{2x}-2^{-2x})\\end{align*}$$\n\n$$\\begin{align*}S+D&=\\frac{1}{2}(2^x+2^{-x}+2^x-2^{-x})\\\\S+D&=2^x\\end{align*}$$\n\n$$\\begin{align*}S-D&=\\frac{1}{2}(2^x+2^{-x}-2^x+2^{-x})\\\\S-D&=2^{-x}\\end{align*}$$\n\n$$\\begin{align*}S^2-D^2&=(S+D)(S-D)\\\\S^2-D^2&=2^x\\times2^{-x}\\\\S^2-D^2&=1\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 80
      },
      {
        "id": "p29-q17-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Rewrite the formulae for $S$ and $D$ as quadratic equations in $2^x$. Hence express $x$ in terms of $S$, and in terms of $D$, in the case where $x>1$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$(2^x)^2-2S(2^x)+1=0$ and $(2^x)^2-2D(2^x)-1=0$.\n\n$x=\\log_2\\!\\left(S+\\sqrt{S^2-1}\\right)$ and $x=\\log_2\\!\\left(D+\\sqrt{D^2+1}\\right)$.\n\nSubstitute $u=2^x$ and select the roots consistent with $u>2$.",
          "worked": "Let $u=2^x$. Since $x>1$, $u>2$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}2S&=u+\\frac{1}{u}\\\\2Su&=u^2+1\\\\u^2-2Su+1&=0\\end{align*}$$\n\nThus the quadratic in $2^x$ is $(2^x)^2-2S(2^x)+1=0$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}u&=\\frac{2S\\pm\\sqrt{4S^2-4}}{2}\\\\u&=S\\pm\\sqrt{S^2-1}\\end{align*}$$\n\nHere $S>1$. The two roots are positive with product $1$, so the smaller root is less than $1$. Since $u>2$, choose the larger root.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}2^x&=S+\\sqrt{S^2-1}\\\\x&=\\log_2\\!\\left(S+\\sqrt{S^2-1}\\right)\\end{align*}$$\n\nSimilarly,\n\n$$\\begin{align*}2D&=u-\\frac{1}{u}\\\\2Du&=u^2-1\\\\u^2-2Du-1&=0\\end{align*}$$\n\nThus the quadratic in $2^x$ is $(2^x)^2-2D(2^x)-1=0$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}u&=\\frac{2D\\pm\\sqrt{4D^2+4}}{2}\\\\u&=D\\pm\\sqrt{D^2+1}\\end{align*}$$\n\nSince $\\sqrt{D^2+1}>|D|$, the minus root is negative and cannot equal $2^x$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}2^x&=D+\\sqrt{D^2+1}\\\\x&=\\log_2\\!\\left(D+\\sqrt{D^2+1}\\right)\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 160
      },
      {
        "id": "p29-q17-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Show that $x=\\frac{1}{2}\\log_2\\frac{1+y}{1-y}$, where $y=DS^{-1}$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\frac{1+y}{1-y}=\\frac{S+D}{S-D}=2^{2x}$, so $x=\\frac{1}{2}\\log_2\\frac{1+y}{1-y}$.\n\nUse the sum and difference from part a, then take logarithms base $2$.",
          "worked": "Since $S=\\frac{1}{2}(2^x+2^{-x})>0$, $y=\\frac{D}{S}$ is defined. From part a, $S+D>0$ and $S-D>0$, so $-1<y<1$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{1+y}{1-y}&=\\frac{1+D/S}{1-D/S}\\\\\\frac{1+y}{1-y}&=\\frac{S+D}{S-D}\\\\\\frac{1+y}{1-y}&=\\frac{2^x}{2^{-x}}\\\\\\frac{1+y}{1-y}&=2^{2x}\\end{align*}$$\n\nTaking logarithms base $2$,\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\log_2\\frac{1+y}{1-y}&=2x\\\\x&=\\frac{1}{2}\\log_2\\frac{1+y}{1-y}\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 74
      }
    ]
  },
